Other posters have already covered the issue of sacrifice value in
reducing god's anger.  There is one other way to reduce your god's
anger: perhaps surprisingly, given what they are, certain actions
which annoy *other* gods will make your god less angry with you.

The drawback to this approach is that the other god will, errr,
demonstrate their annoyance in the form of doing something bad to you.
(I like to call this "smiting" for brevity and lack of a better term.)

The options are:

Sacrifice a unicorn not of your alignment at an altar of its own
alignment "Such an action is an insult to <alignment>!"  You'll lose 1
WIS, lose all protection, the altar's god will "smite" you (see the
table for praying when prayer timeout hasn't expired), and your god's
anger will be reduced by 1.

Sacrifice a former pet at an altar not of your own alignment.  -3
alignment, gain aggravate monster, lose all protection, the altar's
god will "smite" you, and your god's anger will be reduced by 1.

Probably due to a bug, in either of these cases the corpse will not be
consumed, meaning you can eliminate an arbitrary amount of anger if
you can handle the repeated other effects.

There is no message to indicate the anger loss, and other actions
which might make other gods smite you don't mollify your own god.

> Yup.  And you'll need to find a co-aligned altar to work at.  Trying
> it at a cross-aligned altar is a bad idea.

Despite the function name in the code, it is perfectly safe to attempt
to convert a cross-aligned altar while your god is angry with you, as
long as your alignment record isn't negative.


